17592186116071 #edb/ref 1061 14766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116071 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "52c020a1-e0a6-5cb2-bf97-12bce02196c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116071 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116215 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116215 #edb/ref 1061 28229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116215 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d6a66ee2-2a89-5925-b760-ad474a57ea5c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116215 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116480 #edb/ref 1064 "When we transform a logarithmic function $y = \\log_a (x),$ its domain might be modified as well.\n\nFor example, the diagram below shows the graph of the transformed logarithmic function $f(x) = \\log_2(x-1).$ What is the domain of $f(x)?$\n\n![](images/f1/f135e52b40df0923dea0bfd58a3255a096cab4ab23faa7c761ac5998fdfdd782.png)\n\nFirst, recall that the domain of $y=\\log_a (x)$ is\n\n$$\nx > 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $f(x),$ we take the above inequality and replace $x$ with the argument (or input) of the logarithm function contained in $f(x).$ In this case, the argument is $x-1,$ so we get\n\n$$\nx-1 > 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & >0 \\\\ x & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\in(1, \\infty).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116480 #edb/ref 1065 2817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116480 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "345c9fab-a753-50d1-8833-12d8b2699fdc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116480 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116527 #edb/ref 1064 "When we transform a logarithmic function $y = \\log_a (x),$ its range is not affected.\n\nFor example, the diagram below shows the graph of the transformed logarithmic function $f(x) = 3\\log_2(x-1)+1.$ \n\n![](images/4a/4a5bad1159efe184b0afc2731ebd6b5dd6149a0a1ba2d530cfdc769ab2acf250.png)\n\nThe range of $\\log_2 x$ is\n\n$$\n\\log_2 x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nBecause the range of the logarithm function is the interval containing all real numbers, horizontal and vertical shifts and stretches do not affect its range. Therefore, the range of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x) \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116527 #edb/ref 1065 4589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116527 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c2975532-f898-5fd3-a901-a0d8f037906f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116527 #edb/ref 1067 "The Range of a Transformed Logarithmic Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234035 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186564385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234035 #edb/ref 1069 "q-4115" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234035 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234035 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the domain of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}-1)-4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234035 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "681f6cce-6a63-504d-a5b9-ef8102cd6098" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234035 #edb/ref 1074 "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{log}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\nx>0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $f(x),$ we replace $x$ with $\\frac{x}{2}-1$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}-1>0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}-1 & >0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & >1 \\\\ x & >2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\in (2,\\infty ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234436 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186564412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234436 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81990" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234436 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234436 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x)-8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234436 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a2fa019a-adf5-5729-8706-0b94847387b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234436 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $y=\\operatorname{log}⁡x$ is $x=0.$\n\nTo find the equation of the vertical asymptote of $f(x)$, we set the argument of the logarithmic term equal to zero, and solve for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234437 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186564446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234437 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81991" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234437 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234437 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $f(x)=10\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234437 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cf68e5b4-2db7-530c-8311-106b0c01b7b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234437 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is $x=0.$\n\nTo find the equation of the vertical asymptote of $f(x)$, we set the argument of the logarithmic term equal to zero, and solve for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234440 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186564423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234440 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81999" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234440 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234440 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the root of $f(x)=2{log}_{2}⁡(x)-6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234440 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0e6ba761-66c0-5454-8a49-a1749f6fb11e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234440 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n2{log}_{2}⁡(x)-6=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{log}_{2}⁡(x)-6 & =0 \\\\ 2{log}_{2}⁡(x) & =6 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(x) & =3 \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(x)} & ={2}^{3} \\\\ x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564003 #edb/ref 1080 "x=\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564003 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564003 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "18d08f42-e36c-5817-bb88-673383f3d8b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564006 #edb/ref 1080 "x\\in (3,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564006 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564006 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1c8bb634-5f7a-526b-b108-ac68b8ec6d79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564008 #edb/ref 1080 "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-4)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564008 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564008 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1e0ed534-4003-583b-b0cb-07a91f0d3599" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564013 #edb/ref 1080 "x=\\frac{1}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564013 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564013 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "20bc18b6-3403-52ad-9d00-895d3898c60d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564015 #edb/ref 1080 "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564015 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564015 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "20d66e1a-2b6a-5c47-a4b1-f400a0483cb3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564033 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564033 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564033 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "294c8be5-9207-557e-bc83-1962a7384a81" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564036 #edb/ref 1080 "x=2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564036 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564036 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2b0b2db5-67a4-567e-9fd8-540f60ee1dba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564040 #edb/ref 1080 "f(x)\\in (0,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564040 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417